{"text": "vos IMPERIAL CHEMICAL INDUSTRIES LIMITED\nCERTRAL TOXICOLOGY LABORATORY\n\nNCI SERIES IT EXPERIMENT\n\nThe recently coupleted Series II skin-painting trials carried out by\n\nthe NCI are noteworthy for the numbers of variables included and the\nextent of the analyses undertaken. \u2018They also represent the first\npublished trials undertaken in the USA of the British substitute\n\nsmoking material, NSM.\n\n. The use of mouse skin-painting trials as a means of comparing the\nrelatively carcinogenicity of different condensates is now established\npractice, but the analysis of such experiments has been the subject of\nextensive investigation. In particular, the influence of intercurrent\nmortality on the final incidence of tumours, and the expression of\n\nrelative tumour-producing abilities of different condensates, has occasioned\n\n: a great deal of statistical analysis.\n\nThe former problem is usually met by an actuarial analysis as used by NCI\nor the classical Yule method generally favoured in the UK. It is generally\nagreed that the best method currently available to measure \"tumorigenicity\"\nis the application of Weibull curves to tumour incidences and where good\nfit is obtained, gauge the response by the 'b\u2019 parameter (Peto R and Lee P X,\nBionetrics 1973 29 457).\n\nApplication of these methods to the NCI Series II results and a comparison\nwith results alreedy obtained in Britain highlights differences in\nperformance of blends of NSM with tobacco which will require explanation,\nparticularly if any value is to be obtained from Series ITI, where the\nexperiments with blended materials is to be repeated.\n\nstandardised results 01252626\n\nAg\n\nA summary of the X\n\nCI results treated by the Yule method and compared with\n\nresults obtained in Britain (Table 1) shows reduced respor.ces in the UK\n\n: : due to reduced dosage used. The dose applicd has been carefully selected\nto avoid the distortion of results which occurs when large doses of\ntobacco condensate are applied to mouse skin and the dose response curve\nhas plateaued\". In this region of the dose response curve (ic doses\ngreater than approximately 250 mg/week) the response may actually reduce\nas the dosage is increased. This appears to be occurring in the NCI\nexperiment, where doubling the dose has no significant effect on the",
